data.content:
Here's the full migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

| Location | Pattern | Issue on .NET 8 |
|---|---|---|
| `_thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `SYSLIB0046` (obsolete in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call that must be cancellable | Can't be interrupted by token alone |
| `while (true)` | No cooperative exit | Loop never terminates cleanly |

## Race Condition in Original Code

The original has a bug: if two items are enqueued rapidly, `_newEvent.Set()` may coalesce (it's a manual-reset event), and the second item is never dequeued until a third arrives. The migrated version fixes this.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Clean shutdown — expected
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline
        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Before | After | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` | `Channel<string>` | Channel is a thread-safe async producer/consumer — replaces all three |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_channel.Writer.Complete()` | Gracefully unblocks the reader |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.CancelAfter(5s)` | Cooperative timeout; no `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `while (true)` | `await foreach` with token | Exits cleanly on cancellation or channel completion |
| `lock` around queue | Removed | Channel handles synchronization internally |

## Key Pitfalls to Watch

1. **Cancellation isn't instant** — `Handle(item)` won't be interrupted mid-execution. If `Handle` is long-running, pass the token into it and check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at safe points.

2. **Dispose the CancellationTokenSource** — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The class now implements `IDisposable`.

3. **`StopAsync` is async** — callers must await it. If you need a sync `Stop()`:
   ```csharp
   public void Stop()
   {
       _channel.Writer.Complete();
       _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
       _runTask?.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
   }
   ```

4. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — the `when` filter ensures we only catch our own cancellation, not someone else's.

5. **The original race condition** — `ManualResetEvent` could coalesce multiple `Set()` calls. `Channel` delivers every item exactly once, fixing this silently.
